{"duration_api_ms":209279,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"4b509a7b-8b16-4377-9eed-f143d350ac3f","total_cost_usd":0.7417199,"usage":{"input_tokens":18,"cache_creation_input_tokens":86590,"cache_read_input_tokens":494997,"output_tokens":17271,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3988},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":86590,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":10212,"cache_read_input_tokens":84772,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1818,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1818},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-5-5":{"inputTokens":351302,"outputTokens":15968,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":13,"costUSD":0.17311419999999997,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":5681,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5-5":{"inputTokens":18,"outputTokens":17271,"cacheReadInputTokens":494997,"cacheCreationInputTokens":86590,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.5686057,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":3988,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":86590,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":27,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"reviewer\":\"literature_auditor\",\"paper_id\":\"bicycle-infrastructure-paper\",\"run_status\":\"ok\",\"summary\":\"I checked the central named prior-study characterizations in the introduction and literature review. Most are supported: Fosgerau et al., Goodman et al., Gilardi et al., Ziems et al., Welch et al., Ferster et al., Roaf et al. and Bartik et al. One characterization is contradicted. The review says each cycling-effects study evaluates \\\"one or a few well-documented projects\\\", but Kraus and Koch (2021) use 736 counters in 106 European cities and Roaf et al. review 78 studies. This matters because the sentence motivates the paper's gap. Three items cannot be verified. The first is the claim that Bartik et al. are the \\\"closest study\\\", which would require proving absence. The second is the broad claim that no dated record of cycling infrastructure changes exists. The third is the Fyhri et al. (2026) design and effect size, because the journal full text returned HTTP 403. The parser-quality output flags structured tables and figures but no body-text problem, so I used page text throughout.\",\"findings\":[{\"id\":\"LIT-001\",\"category\":\"mischaracterization_of_prior_work\",\"finding_summary\":\"The review claims that each cited cycling-effects study evaluates only one or a few well-documented projects, but Kraus and Koch (2021) analyse pop-up lanes in 106 European cities and Roaf et al. (2024) is a review of 78 studies.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"medium\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"location\":{\"page\":2,\"page_label\":\"2\",\"section\":\"2 Literature Review, Effects of cycling infrastructure\",\"text_quote\":\"Moreover, each of the studies above evaluates one or a few well-documented projects.\",\"precision\":\"exact\"},\"claim_text\":\"Each of the studies cited under 'Effects of cycling infrastructure' (Goodman et al. 2014; Kraus and Koch 2021; Fyhri et al. 2026; Roaf et al. 2024) evaluates one or a few well-documented projects, so studying many smaller openings requires opening dates that are rarely recorded systematically.\",\"assessment\":\"no\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"The sentence follows a list that includes Kraus and Koch (2021) and Roaf et al. (2024). The abstract and indexing records of Kraus and Koch describe a generalized difference-in-differences design using daily counts from 736 bicycle counters in 106 European cities, matched to data on announced and completed pop-up bike lane projects. They report an average of 11.5 km of provisional lanes built per city within four months. That is a multi-city, many-project design, not one or a few projects. The records I retrieved do not give the number of projects or how the dates were sourced, so I do not infer how Kraus and Koch obtained their dates. Roaf et al. (2024) is a systematic review; the abstract summary reports 72 of 78 studies at medium or high risk of bias, so 'each of the studies' does not fit a review either. Goodman et al. (2014) studies three UK sites (Cardiff, Kenilworth and Southampton), and Fosgerau et al. (2023) is a network-level GPS simulation. The paper's broader point that many small openings need dated records can still hold, but the stated premise is overgeneralized. The check used the versions available by the manuscript date: PNAS 2021 for Kraus and Koch and the 2024 journal article for Roaf et al. I read abstract-level records and did not retrieve the full texts, so the project count and date sources in Kraus and Koch remain unchecked.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"M1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Page 2 literature review\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_002.md\",\"page\":2,\"page_label\":\"2\",\"section\":\"2 Literature Review\",\"text_quote\":\"Moreover, each of the studies above evaluates one or a few well-documented projects.\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-c45862491200db070adbb6ed\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Quote found in page 2 text, line-wrapped in the source.\"},{\"id\":\"E1\",\"type\":\"external\",\"label\":\"Kraus and Koch (2021), PNAS 118(15), abstract record (TU Berlin DepositOnce)\",\"path\":null,\"page\":null,\"page_label\":null,\"section\":\"Abstract\",\"text_quote\":null,\"url\":\"https://depositonce.tu-berlin.de/items/5f818b20-0615-48ab-a3bd-9d97c7da5b6a/full\",\"object_id\":null,\"correspondence\":\"retrieved_external\",\"verification_note\":\"Retrieved the repository record. It states 736 bicycle counters in 106 European cities, a generalized difference-in-differences design, an average of 11.5 km of pop-up lanes per city, and a 11-48% increase in cycling. The full PDF was not read, and the number of pop-up projects and how their dates were sourced were not available.\"},{\"id\":\"E2\",\"type\":\"external\",\"label\":\"Roaf et al. (2024), Journal of Transport & Health 38, 101860\",\"path\":null,\"page\":null,\"page_label\":null,\"section\":null,\"text_quote\":null,\"url\":\"https://westminsterresearch.westminster.ac.uk/item/wqywx/interventions-to-increase-active-travel-a-systematic-review\",\"object_id\":null,\"correspondence\":\"url_only\",\"verification_note\":\"Located through search results only. The 72/78 risk-of-bias figure comes from a search summary, and the full text was not read.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"Each cited study evaluates one or a few well-documented projects\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"M1\",\"E1\"],\"relation\":\"contradicts\",\"note\":\"Kraus and Koch cover 106 cities and 736 counters, with pop-up lane projects in many cities.\"},{\"claim_text\":\"Each cited study evaluates one or a few well-documented projects\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"M1\",\"E2\"],\"relation\":\"partial\",\"note\":\"Roaf et al. is a systematic review of many studies. Details were not verified from the full text.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"Restrict the sentence to the studies it describes (for example the Goodman and Fyhri evaluations of specific routes). Describe Kraus and Koch as a multi-city study of many pop-up lanes, and Roaf et al. as a review. Then explain what remains missing for causal designs, such as dated records of permanent openings outside well-documented programmes.\"},{\"id\":\"LIT-002\",\"category\":\"novelty_positioning\",\"finding_summary\":\"The claim that Bartik et al. (2026) is the closest study cannot be verified, and the review cites no work on LLM extraction of dated events from news archives.\",\"issue_type\":\"cannot_verify\",\"severity\":\"low\",\"confidence\":\"low\",\"location\":{\"page\":3,\"page_label\":\"3\",\"section\":\"2 Literature Review, Newspapers and LLMs as measurement tools\",\"text_quote\":\"The closest study to ours is Bartik et al. (2026)\",\"precision\":\"exact\"},\"claim_text\":\"The closest study to ours is Bartik et al. (2026), which uses LLMs to classify zoning rules from municipal codes; the present paper differs by searching a news archive for events and building a dated, georeferenced dataset of infrastructure changes.\",\"assessment\":\"cannot_verify\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":\"Showing that no closer study exists would require proving absence across the LLM, news and event-extraction literatures. The retrieval available here was limited to search results and abstract-level pages.\",\"evidence_summary\":\"The characterization of Bartik et al. is consistent with the NBER conference version dated 24 April 2026. That version describes LLM classification of regulatory documents and a 96% accuracy rate on binary questions against human-coded data. The paper's contrast with its own task (event search in news, not classification of a known document set) is explicit and qualified. A search surfaced LLM-based extraction from local news in other domains: a flood-mention and geocoding study of Caribbean newspapers, and an Italian road-hazard extraction study (CEUR-WS Vol-4307, paper 29, ParmaToday articles). I could not retrieve full texts to check whether they extract dated events or are closer than Bartik et al. This is not evidence of overstated novelty. It indicates that the section's literature on LLM news extraction is thin and that its 'closest study' label is not supported by a survey of that literature. No prior study is shown to cover the paper's specific task.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"M1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Page 3 literature review\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_003.md\",\"page\":3,\"page_label\":\"3\",\"section\":\"2 Literature Review\",\"text_quote\":\"The closest study to ours is Bartik et al. (2026)\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-03b644fd8d90eae779e2345d\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Quote found in page 3 text.\"},{\"id\":\"E1\",\"type\":\"external\",\"label\":\"Bartik, Gupta and Milo (2026), NBER conference version\",\"path\":null,\"page\":null,\"page_label\":null,\"section\":null,\"text_quote\":null,\"url\":\"https://conference.nber.org/conf_papers/f233049.pdf\",\"object_id\":null,\"correspondence\":\"url_only\",\"verification_note\":\"The PDF fetch returned unreadable binary. The 96% binary-accuracy figure comes from a search summary, so the full text was not checked.\"},{\"id\":\"E2\",\"type\":\"external\",\"label\":\"Parma road-hazard extraction from local news (CEUR-WS Vol-4307, paper 29)\",\"path\":null,\"page\":null,\"page_label\":null,\"section\":null,\"text_quote\":null,\"url\":\"https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-4307/paper29.pdf\",\"object_id\":null,\"correspondence\":\"url_only\",\"verification_note\":\"Seen only in search results. The full text was not read.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"Bartik et al. is the closest study\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"M1\",\"E1\",\"E2\"],\"relation\":\"cannot_verify\",\"note\":\"Closeness depends on an exhaustive literature comparison, which is not possible here. Other LLM-on-local-news extraction studies exist in other domains.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"Soften 'the closest study' to 'a closely related methodological study'. Add one or two works on LLM or NLP event extraction from news in transport, infrastructure or environmental settings, and say how the present task differs.\"},{\"id\":\"LIT-003\",\"category\":\"novelty_positioning\",\"finding_summary\":\"The assertion that a dated record of cycling infrastructure changes is missing cannot be fully verified, and the review does not discuss work that tracks OpenStreetMap cycling-network change over time.\",\"issue_type\":\"cannot_verify\",\"severity\":\"low\",\"confidence\":\"low\",\"location\":{\"page\":3,\"page_label\":\"3\",\"section\":\"2 Literature Review, Data on cycling infrastructure and cycling\",\"text_quote\":\"Detailed data on cycling therefore exist, but a dated record of infrastructure changes to link them to is missing.\",\"precision\":\"exact\"},\"claim_text\":\"Existing data sources mostly record where infrastructure is, not when it was built, and a dated record of infrastructure changes is missing.\",\"assessment\":\"cannot_verify\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":\"A claim that no dated record exists is a claim of absence across many national and local sources. It cannot be proven or contradicted with a finite set of concrete studies in this review.\",\"evidence_summary\":\"The manuscript itself qualifies OpenStreetMap: it says the data record when a feature was added, not necessarily when it was built (citing Ferster et al. 2020, whose abstract I checked only via a search record). It also lists 'historical OpenStreetMap records' as a planned comparison in Section 8. I found work that analyses OSM cycling networks over time, such as the AGILE 2023 study of cycling-network change in OSM in the Loire Valley (abstract retrieved: it analyses change frequency, from a freshness perspective, not opening dates). A search also returned 2026 conference abstracts on OSM change detection for cycling infrastructure, which I did not read. None of these shows that opening dates are available. The point is that the manuscript does not engage with the OSM-history literature when it makes the gap claim. The manuscript's supporting statements are qualified, and the gap claim is plausible. I do not assert that the novelty claim is false.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"M1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Page 3 data on cycling infrastructure\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_003.md\",\"page\":3,\"page_label\":\"3\",\"section\":\"2 Literature Review\",\"text_quote\":\"Detailed data on cycling therefore exist, but a dated record of infrastructure changes to link them to is missing.\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-03b644fd8d90eae779e2345d\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Quote found on page 3.\"},{\"id\":\"E1\",\"type\":\"external\",\"label\":\"AGILE 2023, analysis of OSM cycling network changes over time\",\"path\":null,\"page\":null,\"page_label\":null,\"section\":\"Abstract\",\"text_quote\":null,\"url\":\"https://agile-giss.copernicus.org/articles/4/3/2023/\",\"object_id\":null,\"correspondence\":\"retrieved_external\",\"verification_note\":\"Retrieved the abstract only. It analyses how often the cycling network in OSM changes (Loire Valley region). It does not address opening dates. The exact year range was not stated in the retrieved text.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"A dated record of infrastructure changes is missing\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"M1\",\"E1\"],\"relation\":\"cannot_verify\",\"note\":\"OSM-history work exists but concerns mapping change and data freshness, not verified opening dates. Absence of any dated record cannot be shown.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"State the gap as 'to our knowledge' and cite one or two studies on OSM edit-history or change detection for cycling infrastructure. Explain why edit timestamps do not substitute for opening dates.\"},{\"id\":\"LIT-004\",\"category\":\"characterization_of_prior_work\",\"finding_summary\":\"The characterization of Fyhri et al. (2026) as a difference-in-differences design with an effect of up to five percentage points could not be checked against the journal article.\",\"issue_type\":\"cannot_verify\",\"severity\":\"low\",\"confidence\":\"low\",\"location\":{\"page\":2,\"page_label\":\"2\",\"section\":\"2 Literature Review, Effects of cycling infrastructure\",\"text_quote\":\"In Norway, Fyhri et al. (2026) use a difference-in-differences\",\"precision\":\"partial\"},\"claim_text\":\"Fyhri et al. (2026) use a difference-in-differences design to show that the new bicycle tunnel in Bergen increased cycling shares by up to five percentage points.\",\"assessment\":\"cannot_verify\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":\"The journal full text returned HTTP 403, and the related TØI report summary was unreadable in retrieval, so the design and the effect estimate could not be compared with the paper.\",\"evidence_summary\":\"Search records confirm the article exists (Transportation Research Part A 203, 104787; authors Fyhri, Weyde, Denstadli, Lunke and Tveit). They also show a related TØI report (2114/2025) on the Fyllingsdalen tunnel, a cycle highway and the Kronstad tunnel, based on four survey rounds (2022-2024), with counts. A search summary of that report says cycling share rose 3-5 percentage points among groups with the largest travel-time savings. It also says counts rose 5% through the Fyllingsdal tunnel while other Bergen count points fell 5%. The manuscript's 'up to five percentage points' is compatible with a subgroup estimate, so it is a qualified statement rather than a clear mismatch. Whether the journal article uses difference-in-differences, and whether the five points refer to the whole population or a subgroup, remains unresolved. I checked the journal article and the 2025 report, not an earlier version.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"M1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Page 2 literature review\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_002.md\",\"page\":2,\"page_label\":\"2\",\"section\":\"2 Literature Review\",\"text_quote\":\"use a difference-in-differences\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-c45862491200db070adbb6ed\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"The phrase is on page 2; the full sentence continues across line breaks.\"},{\"id\":\"E1\",\"type\":\"external\",\"label\":\"TØI report 2114/2025 English summary (Fyllingsdalen tunnel)\",\"path\":null,\"page\":null,\"page_label\":null,\"section\":null,\"text_quote\":null,\"url\":\"https://www.toi.no/getfile.php/1379820-1758720629/Publikasjoner/T%C3%98I%20rapporter/2025/2114-2025/2114-2025_Summary.pdf\",\"object_id\":null,\"correspondence\":\"url_only\",\"verification_note\":\"The PDF fetch returned unreadable binary. Figures come from a search summary and the text was garbled in places.\"},{\"id\":\"E2\",\"type\":\"external\",\"label\":\"Fyhri et al. (2026), Transportation Research Part A 203, 104787\",\"path\":null,\"page\":null,\"page_label\":null,\"section\":null,\"text_quote\":null,\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0965856425004203\",\"object_id\":null,\"correspondence\":\"url_only\",\"verification_note\":\"HTTP 403, so the full text was not read.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"Difference-in-differences design with up to five percentage point increase in cycling share\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"M1\",\"E1\",\"E2\"],\"relation\":\"cannot_verify\",\"note\":\"A related report suggests subgroup effects of 3-5 percentage points, but the journal article's design and estimand were not read.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"Confirm against the published article that the design is difference-in-differences. State whether the five-point estimate refers to the full population or to groups with the largest time savings.\"}],\"notes\":[\"Retrieval was limited to abstracts, repository records and search summaries. Full texts were not read for Kraus and Koch, Roaf et al., Bartik et al., Fyhri et al. or Raudszus et al.\",\"Supported or unproblematic characterizations: Fosgerau et al. (2023), Goodman et al. (2014), Gilardi et al. (2023), Ziems et al. (2024), Welch et al. (2016) and Ferster et al. (2020).\",\"Fosgerau et al.'s 60% figure is a simulation against a counterfactual without the lane network. The manuscript says 'estimated', which is consistent.\",\"Ziems et al. (arXiv abstract): 13 models on 25 English CSS benchmarks, fair agreement with humans on classification, and LLMs recommended as annotators on human teams. This matches the manuscript.\",\"Earl et al. (2004) was not retrieved. The selection-bias and description-bias characterization is a standard, well-known description of that review, but it was not verified here.\",\"Several cited items carry 2026 dates (Klein et al., Quintero-Gomez and Calvo-Bascones, European Commission, Raudszus et al.). I did not verify them individually, and the Raudszus Dresden characterization was not verified (HTTP 403).\"],\"completion\":{\"version\":1,\"state\":\"complete\",\"scope_description\":\"Literature audit of the introduction and literature review of the 18-page manuscript. I covered novelty and gap claims, the closest-study positioning, and the named characterizations of Fosgerau, Goodman, Kraus and Koch, Fyhri, Roaf, Ferster, Raudszus, Gibbons and Machin, Welch, Earl, Gilardi, Ziems and Bartik.\",\"checks_completed\":[\"Read parser-quality output and used page text for all manuscript evidence\",\"Read the full manuscript text including appendices\",\"Compared the Kraus and Koch, Roaf and Goodman characterizations with retrieved abstract-level sources\",\"Checked the Fosgerau, Gilardi, Ziems, Welch and Ferster characterizations against retrieved abstracts or search records\",\"Attempted retrieval of Fyhri et al., Bartik et al. and Raudszus et al.; two of the three fetches failed or returned binary\",\"Searched for prior OSM-history and LLM news-extraction literature relevant to the novelty claims\"],\"missing_checks\":[],\"evidence_limitations\":[\"Journal full texts for Fyhri et al. and Raudszus et al. returned HTTP 403\",\"The Bartik et al. NBER PDF and the TØI report PDF were unreadable in retrieval\",\"Kraus and Koch, Roaf et al., Welch et al. and Ferster et al. were checked at abstract or index level only\",\"Earl et al. (2004) and Gibbons and Machin (2005) were not retrieved\",\"The completeness of the literature on OSM history and LLM news extraction could not be established, so absence of prior work was not asserted\"]},\"coverage\":[{\"item_id\":\"assigned_remit\",\"source_object_ids\":[],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Read the manuscript and parser-quality output, then checked the central named prior-study characterizations and novelty claims against retrieved external sources.\",\"status\":\"linked_finding\",\"reason\":\"One contradicted characterization (LIT-001) and three cannot_verify items (LIT-002 to LIT-004). Retrieval limits are in the completion record.\",\"finding_ids\":[\"LIT-001\",\"LIT-002\",\"LIT-003\",\"LIT-004\"],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"sampled\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:fosgerau-60pct\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-a50e96cda8f251a4eabe3e71\"],\"claim\":\"In Copenhagen, the bicycle lane network is estimated to have increased the number of bicycle trips by 60 percent (Fosgerau et al. 2023).\",\"check_performed\":\"Compared with the Fosgerau et al. abstract and search records: a 60% increase in bicycle trips and 90% in bicycle kilometres versus a counterfactual without the network.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"The figure matches. The manuscript's word 'estimated' fits a simulation-based counterfactual.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:kraus-koch-and-goodman-before-after\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-c45862491200db070adbb6ed\"],\"claim\":\"Goodman et al. (2014) and Kraus and Koch (2021) compare outcomes before and after routes open and show large increases in cycling.\",\"check_performed\":\"Compared with the iConnect abstract (proximity to new routes predicting more walking and cycling at 2 years, none at 1 year) and the Kraus and Koch abstract (difference-in-differences, 11-48% increase in cycling).\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"Both characterizations match the retrieved abstracts. Goodman's exposure is distance to the new routes, and the manuscript describes it that way.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:each-study-few-projects\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-c45862491200db070adbb6ed\"],\"claim\":\"Each of the studies above evaluates one or a few well-documented projects.\",\"check_performed\":\"Compared the generalization with the scope of Kraus and Koch (106 cities, 736 counters) and Roaf et al. (a systematic review).\",\"status\":\"linked_finding\",\"reason\":\"The generalization conflicts with the scope of at least two of the cited studies.\",\"finding_ids\":[\"LIT-001\"],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:fyhri-did\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-c45862491200db070adbb6ed\"],\"claim\":\"Fyhri et al. (2026) use a difference-in-differences design and find an increase of up to five percentage points in cycling shares.\",\"check_performed\":\"Attempted retrieval of the journal article (HTTP 403) and the related TØI report summary (binary). Used search records for the report.\",\"status\":\"linked_finding\",\"reason\":\"The design and estimand could not be confirmed from the article.\",\"finding_ids\":[\"LIT-004\"],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:roaf-risk-of-bias\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-c45862491200db070adbb6ed\"],\"claim\":\"A systematic review by Roaf et al. (2024) finds that most such studies have a medium or high risk of bias.\",\"check_performed\":\"Compared with a search summary of the review's appraisal: 72 of 78 studies at medium or high risk of bias.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"The claim is consistent with the summary. The full text was not read, and the review covers interventions broader than cycling infrastructure.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"peripheral\",\"scope\":\"sampled\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:data-gap-ferster-raudszus\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-03b644fd8d90eae779e2345d\"],\"claim\":\"Existing data sources mostly record where infrastructure is, not when it was built; OSM records when a feature was added; a dated record of infrastructure changes is missing.\",\"check_performed\":\"Checked Ferster et al. (2020) via search records, searched for OSM-history literature, and retrieved the AGILE 2023 abstract. Raudszus et al. (2025) could not be retrieved (HTTP 403).\",\"status\":\"linked_finding\",\"reason\":\"The gap claim is plausible but is an absence claim. The Ferster characterization matches. The Raudszus characterization is unverified.\",\"finding_ids\":[\"LIT-003\"],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"sampled\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:bartik-closest\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-03b644fd8d90eae779e2345d\"],\"claim\":\"The closest study to ours is Bartik et al. (2026), who use LLMs to classify zoning rules and reach 96 percent accuracy on binary questions.\",\"check_performed\":\"Compared the description with search-retrieved excerpts of the NBER conference version (24 April 2026). Searched for other LLM news-extraction work.\",\"status\":\"linked_finding\",\"reason\":\"The description is consistent with the excerpts. The 'closest' label cannot be verified.\",\"finding_ids\":[\"LIT-002\"],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:gilardi-ziems-earl\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-03b644fd8d90eae779e2345d\"],\"claim\":\"Gilardi et al. show ChatGPT outperforms crowd workers on annotation tasks including relevance; Ziems et al. find LLMs do not beat the best fine-tuned models on classification but reach fair agreement and should annotate alongside humans; Earl et al. identify selection and description bias.\",\"check_performed\":\"Compared with the Gilardi abstract and body summary, the Ziems arXiv abstract (25 benchmarks) and search records.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"Gilardi and Ziems match. Earl et al. was not retrieved, so that part is not verified.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"peripheral\",\"scope\":\"sampled\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:welch-gibbons-machin\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-03b644fd8d90eae779e2345d\"],\"claim\":\"Welch et al. (2016) find access to off-street multi-use paths associated with higher prices and proximity to on-street bike lanes with lower prices; Gibbons and Machin (2005) use new station openings to value rail access.\",\"check_performed\":\"Compared the Welch characterization with the EconPapers abstract summary. Did not retrieve Gibbons and Machin.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"The Welch characterization matches. Gibbons and Machin is a well-known study cited for a standard description, but it was not retrieved here.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"peripheral\",\"scope\":\"sampled\"}]}","structured_output":{"reviewer":"literature_auditor","paper_id":"bicycle-infrastructure-paper","run_status":"ok","summary":"I checked the central named prior-study characterizations in the introduction and literature review. Most are supported: Fosgerau et al., Goodman et al., Gilardi et al., Ziems et al., Welch et al., Ferster et al., Roaf et al. and Bartik et al. One characterization is contradicted. The review says each cycling-effects study evaluates \"one or a few well-documented projects\", but Kraus and Koch (2021) use 736 counters in 106 European cities and Roaf et al. review 78 studies. This matters because the sentence motivates the paper's gap. Three items cannot be verified. The first is the claim that Bartik et al. are the \"closest study\", which would require proving absence. The second is the broad claim that no dated record of cycling infrastructure changes exists. The third is the Fyhri et al. (2026) design and effect size, because the journal full text returned HTTP 403. The parser-quality output flags structured tables and figures but no body-text problem, so I used page text throughout.","findings":[{"id":"LIT-001","category":"mischaracterization_of_prior_work","finding_summary":"The review claims that each cited cycling-effects study evaluates only one or a few well-documented projects, but Kraus and Koch (2021) analyse pop-up lanes in 106 European cities and Roaf et al. (2024) is a review of 78 studies.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"medium","confidence":"medium","location":{"page":2,"page_label":"2","section":"2 Literature Review, Effects of cycling infrastructure","text_quote":"Moreover, each of the studies above evaluates one or a few well-documented projects.","precision":"exact"},"claim_text":"Each of the studies cited under 'Effects of cycling infrastructure' (Goodman et al. 2014; Kraus and Koch 2021; Fyhri et al. 2026; Roaf et al. 2024) evaluates one or a few well-documented projects, so studying many smaller openings requires opening dates that are rarely recorded systematically.","assessment":"no","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"The sentence follows a list that includes Kraus and Koch (2021) and Roaf et al. (2024). The abstract and indexing records of Kraus and Koch describe a generalized difference-in-differences design using daily counts from 736 bicycle counters in 106 European cities, matched to data on announced and completed pop-up bike lane projects. They report an average of 11.5 km of provisional lanes built per city within four months. That is a multi-city, many-project design, not one or a few projects. The records I retrieved do not give the number of projects or how the dates were sourced, so I do not infer how Kraus and Koch obtained their dates. Roaf et al. (2024) is a systematic review; the abstract summary reports 72 of 78 studies at medium or high risk of bias, so 'each of the studies' does not fit a review either. Goodman et al. (2014) studies three UK sites (Cardiff, Kenilworth and Southampton), and Fosgerau et al. (2023) is a network-level GPS simulation. The paper's broader point that many small openings need dated records can still hold, but the stated premise is overgeneralized. The check used the versions available by the manuscript date: PNAS 2021 for Kraus and Koch and the 2024 journal article for Roaf et al. I read abstract-level records and did not retrieve the full texts, so the project count and date sources in Kraus and Koch remain unchecked.","source_objects":[{"id":"M1","type":"text","label":"Page 2 literature review","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_002.md","page":2,"page_label":"2","section":"2 Literature Review","text_quote":"Moreover, each of the studies above evaluates one or a few well-documented projects.","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-c45862491200db070adbb6ed","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Quote found in page 2 text, line-wrapped in the source."},{"id":"E1","type":"external","label":"Kraus and Koch (2021), PNAS 118(15), abstract record (TU Berlin DepositOnce)","path":null,"page":null,"page_label":null,"section":"Abstract","text_quote":null,"url":"https://depositonce.tu-berlin.de/items/5f818b20-0615-48ab-a3bd-9d97c7da5b6a/full","object_id":null,"correspondence":"retrieved_external","verification_note":"Retrieved the repository record. It states 736 bicycle counters in 106 European cities, a generalized difference-in-differences design, an average of 11.5 km of pop-up lanes per city, and a 11-48% increase in cycling. The full PDF was not read, and the number of pop-up projects and how their dates were sourced were not available."},{"id":"E2","type":"external","label":"Roaf et al. (2024), Journal of Transport & Health 38, 101860","path":null,"page":null,"page_label":null,"section":null,"text_quote":null,"url":"https://westminsterresearch.westminster.ac.uk/item/wqywx/interventions-to-increase-active-travel-a-systematic-review","object_id":null,"correspondence":"url_only","verification_note":"Located through search results only. The 72/78 risk-of-bias figure comes from a search summary, and the full text was not read."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"Each cited study evaluates one or a few well-documented projects","source_object_ids":["M1","E1"],"relation":"contradicts","note":"Kraus and Koch cover 106 cities and 736 counters, with pop-up lane projects in many cities."},{"claim_text":"Each cited study evaluates one or a few well-documented projects","source_object_ids":["M1","E2"],"relation":"partial","note":"Roaf et al. is a systematic review of many studies. Details were not verified from the full text."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"Restrict the sentence to the studies it describes (for example the Goodman and Fyhri evaluations of specific routes). Describe Kraus and Koch as a multi-city study of many pop-up lanes, and Roaf et al. as a review. Then explain what remains missing for causal designs, such as dated records of permanent openings outside well-documented programmes."},{"id":"LIT-002","category":"novelty_positioning","finding_summary":"The claim that Bartik et al. (2026) is the closest study cannot be verified, and the review cites no work on LLM extraction of dated events from news archives.","issue_type":"cannot_verify","severity":"low","confidence":"low","location":{"page":3,"page_label":"3","section":"2 Literature Review, Newspapers and LLMs as measurement tools","text_quote":"The closest study to ours is Bartik et al. (2026)","precision":"exact"},"claim_text":"The closest study to ours is Bartik et al. (2026), which uses LLMs to classify zoning rules from municipal codes; the present paper differs by searching a news archive for events and building a dated, georeferenced dataset of infrastructure changes.","assessment":"cannot_verify","cannot_verify_reason":"Showing that no closer study exists would require proving absence across the LLM, news and event-extraction literatures. The retrieval available here was limited to search results and abstract-level pages.","evidence_summary":"The characterization of Bartik et al. is consistent with the NBER conference version dated 24 April 2026. That version describes LLM classification of regulatory documents and a 96% accuracy rate on binary questions against human-coded data. The paper's contrast with its own task (event search in news, not classification of a known document set) is explicit and qualified. A search surfaced LLM-based extraction from local news in other domains: a flood-mention and geocoding study of Caribbean newspapers, and an Italian road-hazard extraction study (CEUR-WS Vol-4307, paper 29, ParmaToday articles). I could not retrieve full texts to check whether they extract dated events or are closer than Bartik et al. This is not evidence of overstated novelty. It indicates that the section's literature on LLM news extraction is thin and that its 'closest study' label is not supported by a survey of that literature. No prior study is shown to cover the paper's specific task.","source_objects":[{"id":"M1","type":"text","label":"Page 3 literature review","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_003.md","page":3,"page_label":"3","section":"2 Literature Review","text_quote":"The closest study to ours is Bartik et al. (2026)","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-03b644fd8d90eae779e2345d","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Quote found in page 3 text."},{"id":"E1","type":"external","label":"Bartik, Gupta and Milo (2026), NBER conference version","path":null,"page":null,"page_label":null,"section":null,"text_quote":null,"url":"https://conference.nber.org/conf_papers/f233049.pdf","object_id":null,"correspondence":"url_only","verification_note":"The PDF fetch returned unreadable binary. The 96% binary-accuracy figure comes from a search summary, so the full text was not checked."},{"id":"E2","type":"external","label":"Parma road-hazard extraction from local news (CEUR-WS Vol-4307, paper 29)","path":null,"page":null,"page_label":null,"section":null,"text_quote":null,"url":"https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-4307/paper29.pdf","object_id":null,"correspondence":"url_only","verification_note":"Seen only in search results. The full text was not read."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"Bartik et al. is the closest study","source_object_ids":["M1","E1","E2"],"relation":"cannot_verify","note":"Closeness depends on an exhaustive literature comparison, which is not possible here. Other LLM-on-local-news extraction studies exist in other domains."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"Soften 'the closest study' to 'a closely related methodological study'. Add one or two works on LLM or NLP event extraction from news in transport, infrastructure or environmental settings, and say how the present task differs."},{"id":"LIT-003","category":"novelty_positioning","finding_summary":"The assertion that a dated record of cycling infrastructure changes is missing cannot be fully verified, and the review does not discuss work that tracks OpenStreetMap cycling-network change over time.","issue_type":"cannot_verify","severity":"low","confidence":"low","location":{"page":3,"page_label":"3","section":"2 Literature Review, Data on cycling infrastructure and cycling","text_quote":"Detailed data on cycling therefore exist, but a dated record of infrastructure changes to link them to is missing.","precision":"exact"},"claim_text":"Existing data sources mostly record where infrastructure is, not when it was built, and a dated record of infrastructure changes is missing.","assessment":"cannot_verify","cannot_verify_reason":"A claim that no dated record exists is a claim of absence across many national and local sources. It cannot be proven or contradicted with a finite set of concrete studies in this review.","evidence_summary":"The manuscript itself qualifies OpenStreetMap: it says the data record when a feature was added, not necessarily when it was built (citing Ferster et al. 2020, whose abstract I checked only via a search record). It also lists 'historical OpenStreetMap records' as a planned comparison in Section 8. I found work that analyses OSM cycling networks over time, such as the AGILE 2023 study of cycling-network change in OSM in the Loire Valley (abstract retrieved: it analyses change frequency, from a freshness perspective, not opening dates). A search also returned 2026 conference abstracts on OSM change detection for cycling infrastructure, which I did not read. None of these shows that opening dates are available. The point is that the manuscript does not engage with the OSM-history literature when it makes the gap claim. The manuscript's supporting statements are qualified, and the gap claim is plausible. I do not assert that the novelty claim is false.","source_objects":[{"id":"M1","type":"text","label":"Page 3 data on cycling infrastructure","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_003.md","page":3,"page_label":"3","section":"2 Literature Review","text_quote":"Detailed data on cycling therefore exist, but a dated record of infrastructure changes to link them to is missing.","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-03b644fd8d90eae779e2345d","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Quote found on page 3."},{"id":"E1","type":"external","label":"AGILE 2023, analysis of OSM cycling network changes over time","path":null,"page":null,"page_label":null,"section":"Abstract","text_quote":null,"url":"https://agile-giss.copernicus.org/articles/4/3/2023/","object_id":null,"correspondence":"retrieved_external","verification_note":"Retrieved the abstract only. It analyses how often the cycling network in OSM changes (Loire Valley region). It does not address opening dates. The exact year range was not stated in the retrieved text."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"A dated record of infrastructure changes is missing","source_object_ids":["M1","E1"],"relation":"cannot_verify","note":"OSM-history work exists but concerns mapping change and data freshness, not verified opening dates. Absence of any dated record cannot be shown."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"State the gap as 'to our knowledge' and cite one or two studies on OSM edit-history or change detection for cycling infrastructure. Explain why edit timestamps do not substitute for opening dates."},{"id":"LIT-004","category":"characterization_of_prior_work","finding_summary":"The characterization of Fyhri et al. (2026) as a difference-in-differences design with an effect of up to five percentage points could not be checked against the journal article.","issue_type":"cannot_verify","severity":"low","confidence":"low","location":{"page":2,"page_label":"2","section":"2 Literature Review, Effects of cycling infrastructure","text_quote":"In Norway, Fyhri et al. (2026) use a difference-in-differences","precision":"partial"},"claim_text":"Fyhri et al. (2026) use a difference-in-differences design to show that the new bicycle tunnel in Bergen increased cycling shares by up to five percentage points.","assessment":"cannot_verify","cannot_verify_reason":"The journal full text returned HTTP 403, and the related TØI report summary was unreadable in retrieval, so the design and the effect estimate could not be compared with the paper.","evidence_summary":"Search records confirm the article exists (Transportation Research Part A 203, 104787; authors Fyhri, Weyde, Denstadli, Lunke and Tveit). They also show a related TØI report (2114/2025) on the Fyllingsdalen tunnel, a cycle highway and the Kronstad tunnel, based on four survey rounds (2022-2024), with counts. A search summary of that report says cycling share rose 3-5 percentage points among groups with the largest travel-time savings. It also says counts rose 5% through the Fyllingsdal tunnel while other Bergen count points fell 5%. The manuscript's 'up to five percentage points' is compatible with a subgroup estimate, so it is a qualified statement rather than a clear mismatch. Whether the journal article uses difference-in-differences, and whether the five points refer to the whole population or a subgroup, remains unresolved. I checked the journal article and the 2025 report, not an earlier version.","source_objects":[{"id":"M1","type":"text","label":"Page 2 literature review","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_002.md","page":2,"page_label":"2","section":"2 Literature Review","text_quote":"use a difference-in-differences","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-c45862491200db070adbb6ed","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"The phrase is on page 2; the full sentence continues across line breaks."},{"id":"E1","type":"external","label":"TØI report 2114/2025 English summary (Fyllingsdalen tunnel)","path":null,"page":null,"page_label":null,"section":null,"text_quote":null,"url":"https://www.toi.no/getfile.php/1379820-1758720629/Publikasjoner/T%C3%98I%20rapporter/2025/2114-2025/2114-2025_Summary.pdf","object_id":null,"correspondence":"url_only","verification_note":"The PDF fetch returned unreadable binary. Figures come from a search summary and the text was garbled in places."},{"id":"E2","type":"external","label":"Fyhri et al. (2026), Transportation Research Part A 203, 104787","path":null,"page":null,"page_label":null,"section":null,"text_quote":null,"url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0965856425004203","object_id":null,"correspondence":"url_only","verification_note":"HTTP 403, so the full text was not read."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"Difference-in-differences design with up to five percentage point increase in cycling share","source_object_ids":["M1","E1","E2"],"relation":"cannot_verify","note":"A related report suggests subgroup effects of 3-5 percentage points, but the journal article's design and estimand were not read."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"Confirm against the published article that the design is difference-in-differences. State whether the five-point estimate refers to the full population or to groups with the largest time savings."}],"notes":["Retrieval was limited to abstracts, repository records and search summaries. Full texts were not read for Kraus and Koch, Roaf et al., Bartik et al., Fyhri et al. or Raudszus et al.","Supported or unproblematic characterizations: Fosgerau et al. (2023), Goodman et al. (2014), Gilardi et al. (2023), Ziems et al. (2024), Welch et al. (2016) and Ferster et al. (2020).","Fosgerau et al.'s 60% figure is a simulation against a counterfactual without the lane network. The manuscript says 'estimated', which is consistent.","Ziems et al. (arXiv abstract): 13 models on 25 English CSS benchmarks, fair agreement with humans on classification, and LLMs recommended as annotators on human teams. This matches the manuscript.","Earl et al. (2004) was not retrieved. The selection-bias and description-bias characterization is a standard, well-known description of that review, but it was not verified here.","Several cited items carry 2026 dates (Klein et al., Quintero-Gomez and Calvo-Bascones, European Commission, Raudszus et al.). I did not verify them individually, and the Raudszus Dresden characterization was not verified (HTTP 403)."],"completion":{"version":1,"state":"complete","scope_description":"Literature audit of the introduction and literature review of the 18-page manuscript. I covered novelty and gap claims, the closest-study positioning, and the named characterizations of Fosgerau, Goodman, Kraus and Koch, Fyhri, Roaf, Ferster, Raudszus, Gibbons and Machin, Welch, Earl, Gilardi, Ziems and Bartik.","checks_completed":["Read parser-quality output and used page text for all manuscript evidence","Read the full manuscript text including appendices","Compared the Kraus and Koch, Roaf and Goodman characterizations with retrieved abstract-level sources","Checked the Fosgerau, Gilardi, Ziems, Welch and Ferster characterizations against retrieved abstracts or search records","Attempted retrieval of Fyhri et al., Bartik et al. and Raudszus et al.; two of the three fetches failed or returned binary","Searched for prior OSM-history and LLM news-extraction literature relevant to the novelty claims"],"missing_checks":[],"evidence_limitations":["Journal full texts for Fyhri et al. and Raudszus et al. returned HTTP 403","The Bartik et al. NBER PDF and the TØI report PDF were unreadable in retrieval","Kraus and Koch, Roaf et al., Welch et al. and Ferster et al. were checked at abstract or index level only","Earl et al. (2004) and Gibbons and Machin (2005) were not retrieved","The completeness of the literature on OSM history and LLM news extraction could not be established, so absence of prior work was not asserted"]},"coverage":[{"item_id":"assigned_remit","source_object_ids":[],"claim":null,"check_performed":"Read the manuscript and parser-quality output, then checked the central named prior-study characterizations and novelty claims against retrieved external sources.","status":"linked_finding","reason":"One contradicted characterization (LIT-001) and three cannot_verify items (LIT-002 to LIT-004). Retrieval limits are in the completion record.","finding_ids":["LIT-001","LIT-002","LIT-003","LIT-004"],"attempted":true,"importance":"central","scope":"sampled"},{"item_id":"claim:fosgerau-60pct","source_object_ids":["OBJ-a50e96cda8f251a4eabe3e71"],"claim":"In Copenhagen, the bicycle lane network is estimated to have increased the number of bicycle trips by 60 percent (Fosgerau et al. 2023).","check_performed":"Compared with the Fosgerau et al. abstract and search records: a 60% increase in bicycle trips and 90% in bicycle kilometres versus a counterfactual without the network.","status":"checked_no_issue","reason":"The figure matches. The manuscript's word 'estimated' fits a simulation-based counterfactual.","finding_ids":[],"attempted":true,"importance":"central","scope":"exhaustive"},{"item_id":"claim:kraus-koch-and-goodman-before-after","source_object_ids":["OBJ-c45862491200db070adbb6ed"],"claim":"Goodman et al. (2014) and Kraus and Koch (2021) compare outcomes before and after routes open and show large increases in cycling.","check_performed":"Compared with the iConnect abstract (proximity to new routes predicting more walking and cycling at 2 years, none at 1 year) and the Kraus and Koch abstract (difference-in-differences, 11-48% increase in cycling).","status":"checked_no_issue","reason":"Both characterizations match the retrieved abstracts. Goodman's exposure is distance to the new routes, and the manuscript describes it that way.","finding_ids":[],"attempted":true,"importance":"central","scope":"exhaustive"},{"item_id":"claim:each-study-few-projects","source_object_ids":["OBJ-c45862491200db070adbb6ed"],"claim":"Each of the studies above evaluates one or a few well-documented projects.","check_performed":"Compared the generalization with the scope of Kraus and Koch (106 cities, 736 counters) and Roaf et al. (a systematic review).","status":"linked_finding","reason":"The generalization conflicts with the scope of at least two of the cited studies.","finding_ids":["LIT-001"],"attempted":true,"importance":"central","scope":"exhaustive"},{"item_id":"claim:fyhri-did","source_object_ids":["OBJ-c45862491200db070adbb6ed"],"claim":"Fyhri et al. (2026) use a difference-in-differences design and find an increase of up to five percentage points in cycling shares.","check_performed":"Attempted retrieval of the journal article (HTTP 403) and the related TØI report summary (binary). Used search records for the report.","status":"linked_finding","reason":"The design and estimand could not be confirmed from the article.","finding_ids":["LIT-004"],"attempted":true,"importance":"central","scope":"exhaustive"},{"item_id":"claim:roaf-risk-of-bias","source_object_ids":["OBJ-c45862491200db070adbb6ed"],"claim":"A systematic review by Roaf et al. (2024) finds that most such studies have a medium or high risk of bias.","check_performed":"Compared with a search summary of the review's appraisal: 72 of 78 studies at medium or high risk of bias.","status":"checked_no_issue","reason":"The claim is consistent with the summary. The full text was not read, and the review covers interventions broader than cycling infrastructure.","finding_ids":[],"attempted":true,"importance":"peripheral","scope":"sampled"},{"item_id":"claim:data-gap-ferster-raudszus","source_object_ids":["OBJ-03b644fd8d90eae779e2345d"],"claim":"Existing data sources mostly record where infrastructure is, not when it was built; OSM records when a feature was added; a dated record of infrastructure changes is missing.","check_performed":"Checked Ferster et al. (2020) via search records, searched for OSM-history literature, and retrieved the AGILE 2023 abstract. Raudszus et al. (2025) could not be retrieved (HTTP 403).","status":"linked_finding","reason":"The gap claim is plausible but is an absence claim. The Ferster characterization matches. The Raudszus characterization is unverified.","finding_ids":["LIT-003"],"attempted":true,"importance":"central","scope":"sampled"},{"item_id":"claim:bartik-closest","source_object_ids":["OBJ-03b644fd8d90eae779e2345d"],"claim":"The closest study to ours is Bartik et al. (2026), who use LLMs to classify zoning rules and reach 96 percent accuracy on binary questions.","check_performed":"Compared the description with search-retrieved excerpts of the NBER conference version (24 April 2026). Searched for other LLM news-extraction work.","status":"linked_finding","reason":"The description is consistent with the excerpts. The 'closest' label cannot be verified.","finding_ids":["LIT-002"],"attempted":true,"importance":"central","scope":"exhaustive"},{"item_id":"claim:gilardi-ziems-earl","source_object_ids":["OBJ-03b644fd8d90eae779e2345d"],"claim":"Gilardi et al. show ChatGPT outperforms crowd workers on annotation tasks including relevance; Ziems et al. find LLMs do not beat the best fine-tuned models on classification but reach fair agreement and should annotate alongside humans; Earl et al. identify selection and description bias.","check_performed":"Compared with the Gilardi abstract and body summary, the Ziems arXiv abstract (25 benchmarks) and search records.","status":"checked_no_issue","reason":"Gilardi and Ziems match. Earl et al. was not retrieved, so that part is not verified.","finding_ids":[],"attempted":true,"importance":"peripheral","scope":"sampled"},{"item_id":"claim:welch-gibbons-machin","source_object_ids":["OBJ-03b644fd8d90eae779e2345d"],"claim":"Welch et al. (2016) find access to off-street multi-use paths associated with higher prices and proximity to on-street bike lanes with lower prices; Gibbons and Machin (2005) use new station openings to value rail access.","check_performed":"Compared the Welch characterization with the EconPapers abstract summary. Did not retrieve Gibbons and Machin.","status":"checked_no_issue","reason":"The Welch characterization matches. Gibbons and Machin is a well-known study cited for a standard description, but it was not retrieved here.","finding_ids":[],"attempted":true,"importance":"peripheral","scope":"sampled"}]},"ttft_ms":2225,"type":"result","duration_ms":147132,"uuid":"af32fe5d-a7a9-40ed-b0e1-04cf159f846e","ttft_stream_ms":1481,"time_to_request_ms":164,"first_content_frame_ms":1481,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-12-08T17:18:28.888Z"}
